    <body>
      <sec id="article-30758.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Uncal herniation is a life-threatening neurologic emergency resulting from elevated intracranial pressure that forces the medial anterior temporal lobe (uncus) through the tentorial incisura into the suprasellar cistern. This transtentorial shift produces mechanical distortion and vascular compromise of critical structures within the tentorial notch, including the midbrain, oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III), and adjacent cerebral peduncles. Clinically, this condition is characterized by ipsilateral pupillary dilation from third nerve compression, contralateral motor deficits due to peduncular involvement, and progressive brainstem dysfunction that may culminate in coma and death. Most commonly precipitated by expanding supratentorial lesions such as traumatic hematoma, intracerebral hemorrhage, tumor, or malignant cerebral edema, uncal herniation represents failure of intracranial compensatory mechanisms and demands immediate recognition and intervention.</p>
        <p>Clinicians participating in this course strengthen their understanding of the anatomic and physiologic foundations of transtentorial herniation, refine their recognition of early clinical and radiographic warning signs, and enhance their decision-making in the emergent management of intracranial hypertension. The activity reinforces evidence-based strategies for medical stabilization, timely neurosurgical consultation, and escalation of care in unstable patients. Participants also improve interdisciplinary communication and coordination in neurocritical settings, ultimately enhancing patient safety, reducing preventable secondary brain injury, and improving neurologic outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Determine appropriate indications for emergent neuroimaging and neurosurgical consultation in patients with suspected transtentorial herniation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Identify characteristic clinical manifestations of uncal herniation, including ipsilateral pupillary dilation, contralateral motor weakness, and progressive brainstem dysfunction.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate early neurologic warning signs of uncal herniation from other causes of acute neurologic deterioration in patients with elevated intracranial pressure.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improve patient outcomes through effective communication and coordinated collaboration among emergency medicine, neurology, neurosurgery, radiology, nursing, and critical care members of the interprofessional healthcare team.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=30758&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=30758">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Uncal herniation represents a catastrophic manifestation of intracranial hypertension and reflects terminal failure of intracranial compensatory mechanisms (see <bold>Image.</bold> Brain Herniation). The uncus, which forms the anteromedial aspect of the parahippocampal gyrus within the temporal lobe, resides in the supratentorial compartment of the cranial vault, specifically within the temporal fossa. Inferior to the tentorium cerebelli lies the subtentorial compartment, which communicates with the supratentorial space through the tentorial notch (incisura).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Critical neurovascular structures traverse this confined aperture, including the midbrain, cranial nerve III, the posterior cerebral arteries, and the superior cerebellar arteries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r4">[4]</xref> The cerebellum occupies the posterior aspect of the notch. When intracranial pressure rises beyond the limits of autoregulatory compliance, brain tissue may shift across rigid dural partitions; uncal herniation occurs when the medial anterior temporal lobe is displaced through the tentorial incisura into the suprasellar cistern.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r5">[5]</xref> This process constitutes a life-threatening neurological emergency and signifies exhaustion of adaptive intracranial buffering capacity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Uncal herniation is the most common clinically recognizable herniation syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Here, there is a passive horizontal distortion (the mesencephalon is twisted) rather than a vertical displacement of the brain. Clinically, an uncal herniation is characterized by a predictable pattern of neuroanatomic compression, including ipsilateral third nerve palsy, contralateral motor deficits from compression of the cerebral peduncle, and progressive brainstem dysfunction.&#x000a0;This condition most commonly arises in the setting of space-occupying lesions, traumatic brain injury, intracranial hemorrhage, or malignant cerebral edema. Rapid recognition is essential, as delays in diagnosis and intervention are associated with irreversible brainstem ischemia and high mortality.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The conceptual evolution of uncal herniation reflects more than a century of clinicopathologic correlation. Jonathan Hutchinson first described unilateral pupillary dilation (Hutchinson pupil), initially attributed to midbrain involvement. Macewen linked pupillary changes to irritation, paralysis, or vascular compromise of the oculomotor nerve, while Bergmann incorrectly localized the phenomenon to the frontal lobe.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r9">[9]</xref> Ropper later identified acute angulation of the third nerve over the clivus at autopsy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r9">[9]</xref> Hill demonstrated transtentorial pressure gradients and tamponade effects at the foramen magnum and tentorial notch in animal models. Adolf Meyer described posterior cerebral artery compression with hemianopia as a false localizing sign.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r10">[10]</xref> Kernohan and Woltman subsequently reported ipsilateral hemiplegia due to notching of the crus cerebri against the contralateral tentorial margin, now known as the Kernohan&#x02013;Woltman phenomenon.</p>
        <p>Jefferson characterized &#x0201c;tentorial and temporal pressure cones,&#x0201d; while Reid and Cone demonstrated hippocampal extrusion compressing the oculomotor nerve in primate studies. McNealy and Plum further delineated central and uncal herniation patterns.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Finally, Miller Fisher and Allan Ropper challenged the notion of an active process, emphasizing a passive mechanical distortion with widening of the ischial space of Bichat instead.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Together, these observations established the modern understanding of uncal herniation as a biomechanical and vascular consequence of intracranial compartment failure, demanding immediate recognition and intervention in contemporary neurocritical care practice.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The causes of uncal herniation include any condition that significantly increases intracranial pressure (ICP) (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Subdural&#x000a0;Hematoma and Uncal Herniation).&#x000a0;Any expanding&#x000a0;space-occupying lesion within the cranium can potentially&#x000a0;place the patient at risk for uncal herniation.&#x000a0;Uncal herniation can occur&#x000a0;following severe head trauma, which may lead to a rapidly expanding epidural or subdural hematoma. In an uncal herniation, there is usually a supratentorial mass lesion that causes an increase in ICP, resulting in the uncus moving toward the tentorial notch.</p>
        <p>There are multispectral factors that can predispose to raised ICP and brain herniation syndrome, such as:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hematoma (traumatic epidural and subdural hematomas, contusions, intracerebral hemorrhage)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Malignant infarction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tumors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infections (abscess, empyema, hydatid cyst)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hydrocephalus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Diffuse subarachnoid hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pneumocephalus (traumatic or postoperative)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cerebrospinal fluid overdrainage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Metabolic-hepatic encephalopathy&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r12">[12]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>There are reports of&#x000a0;chronic uncal herniation in children with Dandy-Walker syndrome or with other cystic cavities in communication with the fourth ventricle following posterior&#x000a0;fossa shunting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The actual incidence of uncal herniation is difficult to determine. Because uncal herniation can occur secondary to traumatic brain injury, it&#x000a0;is essential to know the incidence of traumatic brain injury (TBI) that can affect an at-risk population. According to the 2015 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention Report to Congress on Traumatic Brain Injury<italic toggle="yes">,&#x000a0;</italic>there are approximately 30 million injury-associated emergency department visits, hospitalizations, and deaths every year in the United States. TBI accounts for 16% of injury-related hospitalizations and one-third of injury-related deaths. In 2010, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention estimated that TBIs accounted for approximately 2.5 million emergency department visits, hospitalizations, and deaths in the United States, either isolated or in combination with other traumatic injuries.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The pathophysiology of uncal herniation is grounded in intracranial volume dynamics as described by the Monro&#x02013;Kellie doctrine. The intracranial compartment contains&#x000a0;3 principal components: brain parenchyma, arterial and venous blood, and cerebral spinal fluid (CSF). According to the Monro&#x02013;Kellie principle, an increase in&#x000a0;1 component necessitates a compensatory reduction in&#x000a0;1 or both of the others to maintain intracranial pressure equilibrium.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>In the setting of a supratentorial mass lesion, such as hemorrhage, tumor, or malignant edema, initial compensation occurs through displacement of CSF into the spinal subarachnoid space and reduction in venous blood volume. As these buffering mechanisms become exhausted, arterial inflow becomes compromised, cerebral perfusion declines, and intracranial pressure rises precipitously. When compliance is fully depleted, brain tissue shifts across fixed dural partitions, resulting in herniation. In uncal herniation, the medial anterior temporal lobe (uncus) is forced over the tentorial notch, compressing adjacent structures, including the midbrain and the ipsilateral third cranial nerve, which lies just medial to the uncus.</p>
        <p>Transtentorial herniation may be divided into&#x000a0;2 types:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Lateral herniation with anterior and posterior subtypes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Central herniation</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The anterior lateral variant is the most common, wherein there is herniation of the uncus, leading sequentially to the effacement of the suprasellar cistern, widening of the ipsilateral perimesencephalic cistern, torsional rotation of the brainstem, Kernohan notch phenomenon, and compression of the posterior cerebral artery. In the posterior lateral variant, the parahippocampal gyrus, the isthmus of the fornical gyrus, and the anterior part of the lingual gyrus are involved. may involve the tectum at the level of the superior colliculus, resulting in Parinaud syndrome. Central herniation causes effacement of the perimesencephalic cisterns, duret hemorrhages, posturing, respiratory depression, and eventually death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>In an uncal herniation, the brain is first squeezed under the tent, into the perimesencephalic cistern. This predominantly occurs in a large type of tentorial notch.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Then, when it crosses the incisura, it compresses the ipsilateral cerebral peduncle and the peripheral part of the oculomotor nerve against the tentorial edge. When bilateral, it involves the parahippocampal and lingual gyri. Compression of the calcarine branches of the posterior cerebral artery may lead to infarction in the same territories.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>The downstream injury pattern reflects both direct mechanical compression and secondary vascular compromise.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;There may be torsional rotation of the midbrain leading to widening of the contralateral ventricular atrium and temporal horn.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Acute hydrocephalus can result from compression of the aqueduct of Sylvius. Descending central herniation can create duret hemorrhages within the brainstem. Dilatation of the contralateral pupil is most likely due to the involvement of the oculomotor nucleus.</p>
        <p>Uncal herniation may, in fact, be a consequence of a passive horizontal shift or a &#x02018;midbraining&#x02019; as described by Fischer.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;This has been validated through autopsy studies that conferred significant correlation between uncal herniation and indentation on the midbrain, but not with the duret hemorrhages.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Uncal herniation represents not merely tissue displacement but also a complex interplay of biomechanical horizontal distortion and vascular compromise,&#x000a0;including contusion, compression, demyelination, and ischemia.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>A patient with an impending uncal herniation will&#x000a0;initially&#x000a0;present&#x000a0;with symptoms of increased ICP. These symptoms include headache, nausea, vomiting, and altered mental status. The physical exam in increased ICP&#x000a0;will demonstrate the Cushing triad of hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations or apnea.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;A late finding on ophthalmologic exam in a patient with increased ICP is papilledema, characterized by blurring of the optic disc margin and decreased venous pulsations. The characteristic signs of uncal&#x000a0;herniation are an acute loss of consciousness associated with ipsilateral pupillary dilation and contralateral hemiparesis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;These symptoms are due to compression or displacement of ascending arousal pathways, the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III), and the corticospinal tract.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A unilateral dilated pupil can be the first&#x000a0;sign to appear on the neurological examination without severe impairment in the level of consciousness or a contralateral hemiparesis. In patients who present with isolated anisocoria, this clinical finding could be a sign that there is an impending uncal herniation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r19">[19]</xref> Over time, the patient may develop impaired ocular motility, leading to a classic down-and-out appearance of the eye.</p>
        <p>The reason for the pupillary dilation from compression of the ipsilateral third nerve is due to the loss of parasympathetic innervation to the eye from compression of the plexus that carries the parasympathetic fibers. With the loss of parasympathetic tone to the eye, the only remaining innervation to the eye is from the sympathetic fibers that travel to the pupil along the arteries, resulting in pupillary dilation. Further compression of the midbrain due to&#x000a0;uncal herniation can progress to lethargy, coma, or death. Ultimately, if untreated, an uncal herniation progresses to a central herniation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The diagnosis of uncal herniation is usually determined by characteristic findings on physical examination and confirmed with brain imaging. Any patient who presents with anisocoria warrants an immediate computed tomography (CT) scan to rule out a life-threatening hemorrhage, brain herniation, or an intracranial mass lesion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;A cranial CT scan is preferred over magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) in the emergent setting because it can be obtained quickly, and CT technology is&#x000a0;readily&#x000a0;available in most healthcare facilities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>When signs of uncal herniation are present, the clinician must act promptly to reduce ICP by adjusting the volume of&#x000a0;1 of the&#x000a0;3 components of the intracranial compartment: brain, blood, or CSF.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Proposed tiers in the management of refractory intracranial hypertension and resultant brain herniation include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Evacuation of mass lesions (hematoma, contusion, infarction, edema, tumors)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Physiological neuroprotection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sedation, analgesics, and ventilation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>CSF drainage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Osmotherapy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyperventilation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypothermia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Barbiturate coma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Decompressive hemicraniectomy&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r22">[22]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Surgical strategies in the management of uncal and other herniation syndromes include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Placement of an external ventricular drain for CSF drainage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evacuation of an extra-axial lesion, such as an epidural hematoma or an acute subdural hematoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Resection of an intracerebral lesion, such as an intraparenchymal hematoma or a brain tumor, causing mass effect</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Removal of brain parenchyma in the form of a temporal lobectomy to decompress the brainstem</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uni or bilateral decompressive craniectomies&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r6">[6]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>
<bold>Differential Diagnosis of Anisocoria and Hemiparesis</bold>
</p>
        <p>In the Kernohan notch phenomenon, patients present with a &#x0201c;paradoxical&#x0201d; ipsilateral motor deficit due to damage to the contralateral corticospinal tract, passing through the cerebral peduncle. This peculiar type of uncal herniation has been described for the first time in an autopsy series for brain tumors in 1927 by Groeneveld and Schaltenbrand, and then in 1929 by Kernohan and Woltman.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;This is due to contusion or focal arterial ischemia of the cerebral peduncle following compression against the tentorial border.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Differential Diagnosis of Pupillary Asymmetry</bold>
</p>
        <p>This list includes:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic or compressive optic nerve neuropathy&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>In compressive third nerve palsy, the pupil becomes fixed and dilated due to paralysis of the sphincter pupillae.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ciliary muscle paralysis also leads to loss of accommodation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>A relative apparent pupillary defect/Marcus Gunn pupil may be observed in traumatic optic neuropathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r33">[33]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Traumatic oculomotor nerve avulsion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r34">[34]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anisocoria
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Mydriasis may result from paralysis of the parasympathetic fibers, such as oculomotor nerve palsy, an Adie tonic pupil, posttraumatic iridoplegia, drug overdose (amphetamine, cocaine), poisoning (belladonna, datura), anticholinergics, sympathomimetics.</p></list-item><list-item><p>On the other hand, miosis may follow lesions affecting the sympathetic pathway or the pupillary dilator, such as Horner syndrome, Argyll Robertson pupils, pontine lesions, opiates, organophosphorus or alcohol poisoning, cholinomimetics, and cholinesterase inhibitors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Other causes of a&#x000a0;dilated&#x000a0;pupil include a posterior communicating artery aneurysm compressing the&#x000a0;oculomotor nerve. Also, medical causes of third cranial nerve neuropathy, such as diabetes (pupil-sparing). However, these disorders typically have preserved&#x000a0;consciousness.&#x000a0;There are multiple causes of reduced&#x000a0;consciousness and coma,&#x000a0;including metabolic and infectious encephalopathy. These, however, typically do not cause focal pupillary dilatation. Although widespread cortical or diencephalic dysfunction can cause pupillary abnormalities, these abnormalities tend to be bilateral. Seizures can also cause transient pupillary abnormalities, observed as hippus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r39">[39]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Differential Diagnosis in Radioimaging</bold>
</p>
        <p>This list includes:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Gyral herniation in tentorial hypoplasia&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r40">[40]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Idiopathic cuneate gyrus herniation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r41">[41]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>An important factor for improving the neurosurgical outcome in patients with uncal herniation is the early diagnosis and timely management of the condition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;The degree of herniation&#x000a0;will also affect the prognosis of patients with uncal herniation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r42">[42]</xref> The reversibility of the herniation becomes more difficult in patients with multiple traumas or when new complications develop as the herniation progresses. However, reversal of an uncal herniation can be achieved, usually only if the&#x000a0;interventions are performed rapidly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;Reversal of uncal&#x000a0;herniation can occur in 50% to 75% of adults with either TBI or with supratentorial mass lesions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Almost 70% of patients showed some motor improvement in the Kernohan notch phenomenon following surgical intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;The duration of pupillary asymmetry is significantly associated with survival.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r35">[35]</xref> Long-term outcomes after successful treatment for uncal herniation may be more favorable in children than in adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Neuroimaging alone is a poor surrogate marker for dichotomising outcomes compared to clinical neurological examination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;Advanced age, significant time delay between trauma and surgery, bilateral dilated pupils, and low Glasgow Coma Scale are bad prognosticators.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r44">[44]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r45">[45]</xref>&#x000a0;Unilateral herniation has a mortality of almost 40%, whereas bilateral herniation has a mortality of almost 90%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Decompressive hemicraniectomy in severe head injury has been shown to increase the risk of vegetative states, unfavourable outcomes, and diminished quality of life when compared to medical management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r48">[48]</xref></p>
        <p>Early detection of anisocoria can be facilitated through:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>ICP monitoring and multimodal management protocol&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r49">[49]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Amplitude-integrated electroencephalography shows voltage suppression, low voltage, and flat tracing almost 1 to 2 hours before the clinical manifestations of cerebral herniation <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r50">[50]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ratio of pupil to limbus diameter&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r35">[35]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Automated pupillometry or ultrasonographic pupillary assessment&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r51">[51]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r52">[52]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Artificial intelligence-integrated models&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r53">[53]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r54">[54]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Herniation of the temporal lobe uncus typically compresses the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve, resulting in ipsilateral mydriasis, and compresses the calcarine branch of the ipsilateral posterior cerebral artery, leading to infarction in the posterior inferior temporal lobe and the medial occipital cortex.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Lateral displacement of the brain may compress the aqueduct of Sylvius, leading to obstructive hydrocephalus. As the midbrain is further displaced, sometimes the contralateral cerebral peduncle is forced against the tentorium edge, creating the Kernohan notch and damaging the contralateral corticospinal tract, thereby resulting in paresis or paralysis ipsilateral to the mass lesion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Hypokinetic-rigid syndrome has also been reported due to damage to the nigrostriatal pathway within the midbrain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r55">[55]</xref></p>
        <p>The most severe complications seen in uncal herniation are coma&#x000a0;or death due to the failure of management strategies to reverse the herniation syndrome. Depending on the amount of tissue involved in the herniation, the patient is also at risk for the development of brainstem or cortical ischemia because of the anatomical proximity of major blood vessels. There will be ipsilateral anisocoria with contralateral motor weakness following an uncal herniation.</p>
        <p>However, the Kernohan notch phenomenon can occasionally cause ipsilateral weakness. With further progression of raised ICP, there will be the lateral shift of the diencephalon, leading to altered sensorium from distortion of the reticular activating system, as well as hydrocephalus from torsion and obliteration of CSF pathways. The onset of downward transtentorial herniation is evident by the presence of decorticate and decerebrate posturing, along with loss of brainstem reflexes. The respiratory pattern progresses through Cheyne-Stokes breathing to hyperventilation, ataxia, and finally apnoea, which eventually leads to fixed, dilated pupils and respiratory arrest owing to compression of the medullary respiratory centers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s12" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>A healthcare team familiar with the signs and symptoms of herniation syndromes should monitor patients presenting with TBI. The neurosurgeon, neurointensivists, and support staff with experience caring for patients with significant neurological injury&#x000a0;are crucial members of the management team.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Deterrence of uncal herniation centers on prevention, early recognition, and aggressive management of conditions that elevate ICP. Because uncal herniation most commonly arises from expanding supratentorial mass lesions&#x02014;such as traumatic brain injury, intracranial hemorrhage, malignant cerebral edema, tumors, or large territorial infarction&#x02014;primary prevention focuses on risk reduction and timely treatment of these underlying pathologies. Strict blood pressure control reduces the risk of hypertensive intracerebral hemorrhage; anticoagulation management and careful monitoring of antithrombotic therapy decrease hemorrhagic complications; and injury prevention strategies (eg, seatbelt use, fall precautions, protective headgear) mitigate traumatic brain injury risk. In hospitalized individuals with known intracranial pathology, adherence to evidence-based neurocritical care principles, including head-of-bed elevation, avoidance of hypoxia and hypercapnia, optimization of cerebral perfusion pressure, early neuroimaging for clinical deterioration, and prompt neurosurgical consultation, can prevent progression from intracranial hypertension to herniation.</p>
        <p>Patient and caregiver education is particularly important for individuals at elevated risk, such as those with brain tumors, prior hemorrhage, hydrocephalus, or recent neurosurgical intervention. Patients should be instructed to seek immediate medical attention for red-flag symptoms suggestive of rising ICP, including worsening headache, repeated vomiting, altered mental status, new focal neurologic deficits, pupillary asymmetry, or progressive drowsiness. Families should be counseled that sudden confusion, unresponsiveness, or unequal pupils constitute neurological emergencies requiring activation of emergency services. For patients recovering from brain injury or surgery, clear discharge instructions regarding medication adherence (eg, corticosteroids, anticonvulsants), activity restrictions, and scheduled follow-up imaging are critical. Early recognition and rapid escalation of care remain the most effective strategies to prevent irreversible brainstem injury and mortality associated with uncal herniation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Other important issues of note include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Uncal herniation occurs when an expanding intracranial lesion prevents the&#x000a0;3 craniocervical components from compensating for increased&#x000a0;ICP.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The characteristic signs of uncal&#x000a0;herniation are an acute loss of consciousness associated with ipsilateral pupillary dilation and a contralateral hemiparesis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anisocoria is a cause for concern, and its cause should be sought expeditiously.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The CT scan is preferred over MRI in the emergent setting because it can be completed quickly, and CT technology is more readily available.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uncal herniation is reversible if recognized early and treated aggressively.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Early management of primary injury and prevention of secondary insults, such as hypoxia, hypotension, seizures, and infection, play a paramount role in managing herniation syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;No single intervention reliably resolves elevated intracranial pressure and associated herniation syndromes. The outcome depends on multimodality monitoring to manage the primary insult and minimize secondary insults. The central dogma in managing raised intracranial pressure is preventing secondary insults. Targeted resuscitation, early specialist management, timely intervention to minimize secondary insults, and target-driven care are the cornerstones of efficiently managing patients with brain herniation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30758.r12">[12]</xref> Patient-centered 'care bundle' by an interprofessional team that includes clinicians, specialty-trained nurses, and therapists, is vital during all stages of diagnosis and care, including the rehabilitative phase, to achieve optimal patient outcomes.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Neurological resuscitation requires close communication between multiple disciplines on the healthcare team to achieve a good clinical outcome. Nurses are the ultimate patient monitors, and recognizing subtle changes in vital signs or the neurological exam may make the difference in patient outcomes. Acute care delivery will require a team of clinicians that includes neurosurgeons, neurologists, traumatologists, radiologists, and critical care specialists, with the ability to communicate effectively, to improve patient outcomes. All team members should be empowered to contribute freely and encouraged to report subtle changes that may affect clinical outcomes. The most effective teams will find a way to discuss patient care and develop a plan that addresses all clinical needs.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30758.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
